# Every column in schema `pm` whose type is `absence_reason`. One row per column: its table and its
# name, as Postgres records them.
#
# The full set, read from what Postgres writes
#   A census that claims every typed blank makes a claim about a set it cannot see from the inside:
#   a relation built from what loaded reports what its arms read, never the column no arm reads.
#   That set is usually declared by hand. Here it does not have to be: `information_schema` is
#   written by Postgres from schema.ddl, so a column added there arrives here with nobody restating
#   it, and nothing in assets/sqlc/ can make it agree.
#
# The type is the test, not the name
#   A column called `absent` and a column called `demand_absent` hold the same kind of fact, and one
#   called `answer` does too; matching on the suffix would miss the first one renamed. `udt_name` is
#   what makes a column a typed blank.
#
# One column is not a question
#   `absence.reason` is every typed blank at once, read from the elements rather than from the table
#   of the thing each is about. It is the census's second route, held against the first by
#   algebra/absences_filed.sqlc; asked as one more question, it would count every blank twice.
-- information_schema.columns, restricted to schema pm and type pm.absence_reason.
SELECT c.table_name::text  AS table_name,
       c.column_name::text AS column_name
FROM information_schema.columns c
WHERE c.table_schema = 'pm'
  AND c.udt_schema   = 'pm'
  AND c.udt_name     = 'absence_reason'
  AND (c.table_name, c.column_name) <> ('absence', 'reason')
